<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> My Family and I</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> It's My Family!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write their own name and other very familiar words without assistance. Interactive: Draw pictures related to a simple sentence they have written.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write other very familiar family-related words without assistance when labeling a family drawing (e.g., "mom," "dad"). Can draw pictures related to simple sentences they have written about family members (e.g., drawing a picture of "my mom" to accompany the sentence, "This is my mom."). Can write familiar family words on cards, without assistance.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards of family members (mom, dad, brother, sister) and names them. Students repeat chorally. Then, the teacher holds up a flashcard, students say the name, and the teacher asks students to "air-write" the first letter of the word (e.g., "M" for mom, "D" for dad). This activates visual and kinesthetic memory for letter formation and connects to the theme "It's My Family!".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes the words "mom" and "dad" on the board clearly, enunciating the /m/ and /d/ sounds. The teacher models writing their own name and says, "This is my name: [Teacher's Name]." The teacher points to each letter as it is written. Then, the teacher draws a simple picture of a mom next to the word "mom" to visually connect the word to its meaning.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to the word "mom" on the board) "Is this 'dad' or 'mom'?" (Expected answer: Mom)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to the drawing of a mom) "Does this picture show a mom?" (Expected answer: Yes)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "My Family and I".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large poster with a drawing of a simple family (mom, dad, brother, sister) and basic labels written next to each member (e.g., "This is my mom. She is friendly.").</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows the family poster. Points to the mom and reads aloud, "This is my mom. She is friendly." Then points to the dad and reads, "This is my dad. He is tall."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher highlights the written words "mom," "dad," "brother," "sister," "She is," "He is," and "my."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher explicitly introduces the target grammatical structures: "Personal pronouns + verb (e.g., 'She is,' 'He is')" and "Possessive adjectives (e.g., 'my mom')".</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher writes the phrase "This is my" and then adds individual family words (mom, dad, brother, sister) to create complete sentences on the board, pointing to each word as it's written and read.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Introducing family members: Use simple sentences to describe family (e.g., "She is my mom."). Describing Family Roles: Using appropriate language to describe family members and their roles (e.g., "This is my mother.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Point to 'mom' on the poster." (Teacher checks by observation)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Show me the word 'dad'." (Teacher points to the word, students point to the word)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Writing familiar family words; using "This is my..." and "She/He is..." with target vocabulary (mom, dad, brother, sister, friendly, tall). Phonemic awareness: Matching letter sounds to words, such as /b/ in "brother" and /s/ in "sister".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice - Labeling):</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with simple outlines of family members (e.g., a woman for "mom," a man for "dad," a boy for "brother," a girl for "sister"). Below each picture, there is a blank line. Students also receive a word bank with "mom," "dad," "brother," "sister." Students trace their names on the worksheet. Then, they write the correct word from the word bank under each picture. The teacher circulates, providing individual help with letter formation and spelling.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled Practice - Sentence Completion):</b> The teacher provides a second worksheet with incomplete sentences and pictures. For example: a picture of a mom with the sentence "This is my ______. She is ______." and a picture of a dad with "This is my ______. He is ______." Students choose words from a provided word box (e.g., mom, dad, friendly, tall) to complete the sentences. The teacher guides students to say the words as they write them to reinforce pronunciation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Pair Check & Share):</b> Students work in pairs. They show their completed sentences and labels from Activity 2 to their partner. One student points to a picture and says, "This is my mom," while the partner reads the sentence they wrote. This allows for peer-checking and reinforces the written words orally.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "My Family and I".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual Written Product: "My Family Card".</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher gives each student a small piece of cardstock.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students choose ONE family member (mom, dad, brother, or sister) to draw on their card.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Below their drawing, students write their own name.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Then, they write a simple sentence about the family member they drew, such as "This is my mom." or "I love my dad." The teacher provides the sentence starters on the board for reference if needed.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The focus is on applying previous writing practice to produce a personal artifact. The teacher reminds students about correct letter formation and remembering the words.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and collection of "My Family Card" for review.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students complete and submit their "My Family Card" from the Production stage.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to write their own name (without assistance).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to write 1-2 familiar family words (e.g., "mom," "dad") without assistance.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Drawing is related to the written sentence.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempt to form simple sentences (e.g., "This is my mom.").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I write my name on the card?" (Students point to their name and give a thumbs up/down).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I read the family word I wrote?" (Students silently read their word).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to write today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What word do I want to practice writing again?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did students successfully write their names and at least one family word? Were the instructions clear enough for 1st graders to follow the writing tasks? What specific challenges did students face with letter formation or spelling? What adjustments are needed for students who struggled with writing familiar words?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will use their "My Family Cards" to introduce their family members to a partner, consolidating the writing with speaking and preparing for a larger class display or family project.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>